{"reference":"Micah 1","book":"Micah","chapter":1,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The opening identifies Micah as a Moreshethite and places his visions about Samaria and Jerusalem during the reigns of Jotham, Ahaz, and Hezekiah. The repeated summons to hear at 1:2, later echoed near 3:1 and 6:1, has led to an influential reading of the book in three broad cycles, though it does not settle every question about changes of speaker or composition.","verse_refs":["Micah 1:1","Micah 1:1-16","Micah 1:2"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_micah_001_e8db4272ee1801d241e14ce4"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"Historical Context","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The chapter's grief over threatened Judah, its towns, and captivity belongs near the imperial warfare reflected in records from around 701 BCE: the Sennacherib Prism records Hezekiah's tribute, while the Lachish reliefs portray Assyrian conquest. These materials illuminate the political and military world in which the chapter's language of devastation is heard.","verse_refs":["Micah 1:8-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_micah_001_6f24634ed79883a157648e74"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"people_places","title":"Places and Lament","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Verses 10-16 are shaped as a lament or dirge: a formal poem of mourning. Its sequence of Shephelah place names is organized with sound-play, so the names themselves contribute to the poem's grieving movement. Several proposed wordplays, exact site identifications, and suggested routes remain uncertain; the passage is clearer as a poetic lament over affected communities than as a precisely mapped itinerary.","verse_refs":["Micah 1:1","Micah 1:1-16","Micah 1:10-16","Micah 1:8-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_micah_001_bdc71e184036784fe0f9b6ef"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Micah as a Book","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"One influential reading sees Micah's final form moving through three large movements—chapters 1-2, 3-5, and 6-7—in which judgment and hope repeatedly alternate. That wider pattern helps place the severe opening of chapter 1 within a book that does not end with its announcement of disaster, while remaining an interpretive outline rather than a solution to every compositional question.","verse_refs":["Micah 2:1-13","Micah 3:1-12","Micah 4:1-13","Micah 5:1-15","Micah 6:1-16","Micah 7:1-20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_micah_001_10270315b1ea649bba63f337"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}